First Author: Johnston
Title: Estimating Calibrated Risks Using Focal Loss and Gradient-Boosted Trees for Clinical Risk Prediction
Source number: 22

Very short summary: This study evaluated a calibration-aware gradient-boosted tree strategy for clinical risk prediction, including post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD) after lung transplantation using SRTR data. Five key findings were: (1) the lung-transplant cohort included 33,244 adult recipients after exclusions; (2) the binary outcome was post-transplant PTLD, modeled at 1, 3, 5, 8, and 10 years; (3) the methodology combined focal-loss gradient-boosted trees with a closed-form calibration transformation (Ψγ) and Bayesian hyperparameter optimization; (4) raw focal-loss models were miscalibrated and systematically overestimated PTLD risk in calibration plots; and (5) calibration improved substantially after Ψγ transformation while maintaining discrimination, with calibrated focal-aware XGBoost achieving AUROC 0.700, Brier score 0.128, and calibration slope 0.968 for 10-year PTLD risk. Tables 1–3 and Figures 2, 5–9 were used directly for this extraction.

