Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The lung-transplant analysis used a large national SRTR cohort and applied transparent exclusion criteria, reducing single-center selection bias.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were structured SRTR variables available in the registry, including demographics, immunosuppression, viral infection history, and HLA data.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The binary outcome was explicitly defined as PTLD, derived from the SRTR malignancy file, with time-to-PTLD calculated from transplant date to diagnosis date.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include repeated 10-fold cross-validation, Bayesian hyperparameter optimization, and detailed calibration assessment. Remaining uncertainty comes from the lack of a fully independent external validation cohort and the absence, in the retrieved text, of a clear missing-data strategy for the SRTR lung dataset.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: This article directly addresses AI-based clinical risk prediction in lung transplant recipients using SRTR data and is highly relevant to the review question.

